ostics  cytology and histology  one of the things we're really proud of  here is just over 97  of our  test requests from our physicians and  all of our client facilities are  performed here in house  and we provide reference laboratory  services for three hospitals  as well as 14 long-term care facilities  so prior to the pandemic we had four  different molecular platforms that we  had employed in our laboratory  we had the cepheid gene expert  on that platform we run mrsa  surveillance  c-difficile  group b strep  as well as a genetic mutation  test for factor two and five  coagulopathies  we also brought in the panther  in 2013  in the panther we have a pretty robust  menu we run gonorrhea chlamydia  mycoplasma genitalium  trichomonas vaginalis  hpv  hpv genotyping and a vaginitis panel  a couple years after that we were able  to bring in the abbott id nows as a  quick molecular screen for flu a and b  rsv and group a strep  and then in 2016 we were fortunate  enough to bring in uh the verigene  platform  on the varagine  platform we run the gram negative and  gram positive blood culture panels as  well as the respiratory flex panel and  the entire  pathogens panel  the benefits that we saw in bringing the  vera gene in  were  a lot towards our antimicrobial  stewardship program  so the blood culture panels  compared to conventional culture allow  us to do directed treatment 28 to 48  hours faster  which is much better for patient  outcomes  the respiratory flex panel  provides us a very robust screening tool  for respiratory pathogens  it provided us a backup for our rapid  flu and rrsp testing  and we had a large amount of border tele  testing  that was coming from peds practices that  we were having to send out  so we were able to bring in  all of these assays under this one  platform and and do quite a bit as well  as with the entire panel and improving  the screening tool we had over  conventional culture  life before the pandemic  in my department we had two to three  ftes on day shift  just depending on the day of the week  and the volume  on the vera gene uh those volumes  included a very steady amount of blood  culture and enteric panels and then of  course the respiratory panel volume  [Music]  varied depending on the season so  fall and winter time we would see a  significant increase in that volume  but that was easily handled in the daily  workflow  at the bottom of the slide you can see  the verizon workflow for those of you  that aren't familiar with platform  it's a very effective tool for a  facility our size  we started off with  just one of the processors and reader  we're now up to three  but you can see on the bottom there it's  a  pretty simple just adding your sample  the different components and letting the  instrument  do the processing and then at the very  last step reading the panels for you  so  as the pandemic started  there weren't very many  assays available to  any facilities  so  all of our testing was either having to  be sent to a state laboratory  or when some of the large commercial  reference laboratories started offering  it  there was places that we could send  covered pcr testing  so initially before we had any  capabilities  we use the verigene  respiratory flex panel as a rule out  test  so in fact the state  lab in order to send a specimen you had  to get authorization  so we would perform the test in-house  with the respiratory flex panel  if no targets were found  and we had a symptomatic patient then we  could get approval to send that specimen  to the state for testing  we did have  two platforms that  were initially available for testing  they were the gene expert and the id now  but because of  the amount of  facilities that use those platforms  it was next to impossible to get a  sufficient inventory to really do any  significant  testing  therefore we went with purchase of an  aries platform  and we started with the aries in doing a  lab develop test  once the lab developed test was fully  implemented  then the eua became available so  we  were going to transition straight to the  eua but because of allocation issues  then  for quite some time we ran both ldt and  eua  four covert on the aries  and the usage was just based on what  product was available that week how much  we could get  so until the large scale production of  the eua  we mixed both ldt and eua in the same  day sometimes  now we are exclusively on the eua assay  what the aries really allowed for us to  do was prioritize patients in our  facility so  we  focused on patients in our er  we focused on getting our surgeries  elective surgeries back operational so  we were able to screen those patients  in-house the day before testing  i'm sorry the day before their surgery  any employees that were suspected of  being infected we were able to run those  and get those results out the same day  for employee health  and then  we've supported  three to four of our local fire  departments in testing  the first responders from those  departments  because of the testing that we were  doing  the indiana department of health reached  out to us  to join a network that they were  creating to help increase the  capacity of testing within the state  so initially they asked nine  laboratories across indiana  to join this network and with that  we were able to get  a higher increase in the allocations of  various reagents  and since we already had a panther  in-house  we were able to bring in  sars testing on the panther as well  as part of that partnership we also  obtained the second aries instrument  that we put into place  so as the volumes continue to increase  we did have a problem with the panther  because  the t-can tips that are used on the  panther  became very hard to get and those were  on allocation so even if i could get  panther  actima reagents i might not have the  tips to be able to run the instrument  so therefore working with the state  department of health or the department  of health  we ended up adding two luminex magpix  instruments  as well as a bmru emag to do the  extractions and then two thermocyclers  as the pandemic raged on and we were  doing all this testing i was able to add  two ftes to the department so we added  an additional fte to day shift  and then one to second shift and we had  not had  anyone but generalist on our second  shift  so we added a molecular specialist onto  our second shift just to keep  the panther instrument flowing and then  run any of those  that we're going to run on the aries  bringing those in at night  the magpix and the panther combination  really allowed us to increase our  capacity  in a major way and reallocate  um the use of the instrumentation so  we run a lot of stuff on the panther so  bringing the magpix in allowed us to  take some of the burden off of that so  we could continue to perform some other  testing  in addition  when we got both of those online the  state started sending specimens  from outside our network to us to run  and a lot of those tests that were being  collected within the county were  actually being ran out of state  because the capacity issues  all around the area so there were a lot  of tests going i believe out to  wisconsin that we were able to keep in  state within the county  and then of course running those other  larger  high throughput platforms allowed us to  really focus more on  the aries as using it for our direct  admits again more pre-surgical screening  employee screening and our first  responders  pandemic stability stabilization we did  have a  time where things were starting to  [Music]  decrease pretty significantly in volumes  as i record this  the last two weeks we have been back at  record volumes so  slide is  chronologically good but um we are  very busy again and back to  um high pandemic levels but  use of these platforms really  uh was helpful when the volumes start to  decrease  so we changed our focus  from doing all the testing that had been  coming in  because it was a much lower volume we  started offering direct consumer testing  for individuals that needed travel  testing  so they could pay upfront  schedule an appointment  and because of the way the aries  platform works we were able to get these  results out same day for these folks  and that's really important because of  the time frames that some of the  countries were requiring  most of them are 72 hours but it kind of  depends on  what connecting flights you have some  people were having to get results back  the same day so that they could make  their flights  it's been a very popular program but it  did have some  some drawbacks  each destination has very specific  requirements so the type of test used  we had to make sure a lot of them  require real-time pcr  the collection methods you know was it  nasopharyngeal  nasal oropharyngeal and what  and then eventually we had to add a qr  code to our reports um  just to satisfy specific requirements  for some countries  kind of  one of the odd things is that  we've sent people with travel testings  all over the globe  and not had any issues with the  exception of hawaii  and hawaii will not accept our results  because we're not  an approved lab on their site  but i just thought that was interesting  because we've been approved by all these  other countries except for a state in  our own  our own country  so moving on from the pandemic hopefully  the surge we're in right now will will  subside and we can start getting back to  normal  but moving on from the pandemic  having these different platforms in  place  or wanting to to really be able to  expand what we're offering  so on the aries i want to increase test  offerings  one thing for certain that i want to add  from there  would be the hsv one and two  we want to increase our panther test  offerings  hiv quantitative as well as hcv and hbv  and then  hope to upgrade our vera gene to the  verite fair gene 2 platform  that should 